    14	
    15	# Design analysis for the combo variant
    16	# -------------------------------------
    17	# a) Concrete behavior:
    18	#    The 29x29 board still contains a 7x7 lattice of 3x3 cell interiors with 1-pixel dividers.
    19	#    The base tiling is checkerboard: parity-even cells use motif A, parity-odd cells use motif B.
    20	#    A source motif S appears only 3-4 times in the input and is the one that must be fractally
    21	#    completed. To make D1 active rather than cosmetic, the input also contains a repeated distractor
    22	#    family D outside the completion block. A typical count profile is A=22, B=18, D=6, S=3. The
    23	#    old task could treat "the rare pattern" as a simple exception against one uniform background.
    24	#    The fused task requires two linked inferences: first rank motif frequencies to pick S, then use
    25	#    S's dense color as a 3x3 macro-mask whose inactive slots must revert to A or B by parity.
    26	# b) Complexity calibration:
    27	#    Only one distractor family is added. That is enough to raise the distinct motif count from the
    28	#    natural checkerboard triple {A,B,S} to four repeated families {A,B,D,S}, so counting matters,
    29	#    but the visual scene stays readable because every cell still contains exactly one motif. The
    30	#    source remains uniquely least frequent, the distractor remains clearly more frequent than the
    31	#    source, and A/B still dominate the board so the checkerboard is obvious to a human.
    32	# c) Hidden assumptions from the old solver:
    33	#    - "majority_pat = max(counts)" fails because inactive cells are not globally uniform.
    34	#    - "len(counts) == 3" from the D6-only variant also fails because D is present.
    35	#    - "all non-source cells participate in the checkerboard" fails because D intentionally breaks
    36	#      the parity lattice outside the completion block. The new solver therefore scores every ordered
    37	#      pair of non-source motifs as a potential checkerboard basis and chooses the unique pair with
    38	#      the fewest mismatches; those mismatches correspond exactly to distractor cells.
    39	#    - Placement search may still rely only on already-shown source cells because distractors never
    40	#      appear inside the 3x3 completion block, so they do not alter the source subset geometry.
    41	# d) Placement legality:
    42	#    The 3x3 source mask is placed at offsets 0..4 inside the 7x7 cell lattice. Source samples are a
    43	#    strict subset of active mask positions. Distractor cells are sampled only from coordinates
    44	#    outside the 3x3 block, so they cannot overwrite future completion slots or clash with source
    45	#    cells. All motifs occupy fixed 3x3 interiors, so overlap is impossible once cell coordinates are
    46	#    distinct.
    47	# e) Visual observability:
    48	#    A and B share a palette and differ by arrangement, making the checkerboard legible. S introduces
    49	#    its own exclusive color and appears a few times, so its self-similar role is visible. D uses one
    50	#    extra exclusive color and repeats more often than S, giving the solver a genuine frequency-sorting
    51	#    step that a human can infer from examples by counting motif families rather than by reading color
    52	#    ids. The source mask is constrained so inactive pixels hit both checkerboard parities, which makes
    53	#    the D6-style parity-aware refill visible inside the transformed region itself.
    54	# f) Visual cleanliness:
    55	#    No overlays are drawn on top of each other. Each cell is exactly one of A, B, D, or S, and
    56	#    divider lines remain untouched. Distractors stay outside the completion block so the central
    57	#    transformed region still reads as "checkerboard plus source expansion" instead of a pile of
    58	#    unrelated motifs.
    59	# g) Change inferability:
    60	#    The color budget stays small: separator, shared, base-exclusive, source-exclusive, and one
    61	#    distractor-exclusive color. That keeps the mapping learnable. Structural cues do the real work:
    62	#    parity explains A/B placement, frequency singles out S against D, and S's own interior pattern
    63	#    explains the macro-scale completion.
